{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "60",
    "case_summary": "A 29-year-old primigravida woman presented to the outpatient obstetric clinic for a routine antenatal checkup. There was no history of dyspnea, chest pain, palpitation, or lower limb swelling. Her vital parameters were within normal limits.\n\nCardiovascular system examination revealed a systolic murmur in the left upper parasternal region.\n\n- ECG findings:  - Normal sinus rhythm with heart rate of 70 beats/min  - Complete right bundle-branch block with normal PR interval  - Fragmented QRS with a notch on the R wave in inferior leads  - No significant ST-T segment changes    - Transthoracic echocardiography:  - Dilated right atrium and right ventricle  - Large 21-mm defect with left-to-right shunt    - Right-sided heart catheterization:  - Normal cardiac output  - Step-up in oxygenation from superior vena cava (71%) and inferior vena cava (77%) to right atrium (84%)  - Pulmonary vascular resistance: 1.8 Wood units    - Transesophageal echocardiogram:  Image mentioned but no specific findings described beyond confirming the defect    - Figure Title: 12-Lead Electrocardiogram  Image Description: Shows normal sinus rhythm with right bundle-branch block pattern and fragmented QRS with notch on R wave in inferior leads\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Ostium primum atrial septal defect\nB. Sinus venosus atrial septal defect\nC. Patent foramen ovale\nD. Ostium secundum atrial septal defect (OS-ASD)\n",
